  "problem": "Given that $r(t)=\u27e84+3{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1\u27e9,$ what is ${r}^{\u2032}(1)?$\n\n${r}^{\u2032}(1)=\u27e8${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$\u27e9$",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=4+3{t}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=6t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(1)=6\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=4{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(t)=12{t}^{2}+6t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(1)=12\\cdot {1}^{2}+6\\cdot 1=18.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(1) & =\u27e8{x}^{\u2032}(1),\\,{y}^{\u2032}(1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86,\\,18\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
